To solve the problem, we need to calculate how many problems Katie had to complete in total, given that she had 7 pages of math homework and 3 pages of reading homework. Each page had 9 problems on it.

### Step 1: Calculate the number of math problems
Katie had 7 pages of math homework, and each page had 9 problems.

$$
7 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems per page} = 63 \text{ math problems}
$$

### Step 2: Calculate the number of reading problems
Katie had 3 pages of reading homework, and each page also had 9 problems.

$$
3 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems per page} = 27 \text{ reading problems}
$$

### Step 3: Add the math and reading problems together
Now we add the problems from both subjects:

$$
63 \text{ (math)} + 27 \text{ (reading)} = 90 \text{ total problems}
$$

### Final Answer:
$$
\boxed{90}
$$

